Every time the AI calls a write-capable tool, the system records a pending row BEFORE the mutation runs. It captures a snapshot of whatever the tool is about to change for damage, that’s the target’s HP and conditions; for “_end adventure,_” it’s the campaign state plus the set of achievement IDs that existed before the tool ran. The snapshot lives in JSONB so it has no foreign-key dependencies that might cascade-delete it. [...] If you’re building on any kind of agent framework LangChain, Agents SDK, a custom dispatcher there’s almost always a single choke point where tool calls resolve. That’s your injection site. You don’t need to modify each tool. You just need to wrap the dispatcher.

typescript  
async function executeTool(name, input, context) {  
 const shouldAudit = hasRevertHandler(name);  
 const invocationId = shouldAudit  
 ? await recordInvocation({ name, input, context }) // snapshot captured  
 : undefined;  
try {  
 const result = await runTool(name, input, context);  
 if (invocationId) await commitInvocation(invocationId, result);  
 return result;  
 } catch (err) {  
 if (invocationId) await failInvocation(invocationId, err);  
 throw err;  
 }  
} [...] ## WAL or Write-ahead log, but for LLMs

The idea is stolen wholesale from database transaction logs. Before your tool mutates state, write a row to an audit table capturing what you’re about to change. If the mutation succeeds, mark the row committed. If you later want to reverse it, read the prestate from the row and put things back.

